// Unit types. Speed is in blocks per second, hp is hit points, trainTime is in seconds and cost is in
// goods (food, wood, stone, iron_ore, coal, iron). trainedAt names the building that trains the unit;
// requires names a tech the player must have researched first; faction (optional) limits the unit to
// one faction. Roles: worker, line_infantry, heavy_infantry, ranged, cavalry, sapper, caster, siege, monster,
// trader (see roles.json for what each is strong and weak against). description (optional) is shown in the game.
// Combat: damage is the hit points one hit takes off (a unit without damage cannot attack), attackTime
// the seconds between hits, range how far it hits (in blocks, from the middle of the unit; more than 2
// makes it a ranged unit), sight how far it spots enemies to fight on its own, and splash (optional)
// hits every enemy that close to the target too. weakTo (optional) lists roles whose attacks deal it
// that many percent more damage, as in { "caster": 50 }. heal (optional) is the hit points it gives back
// to the most hurt ally within healRange blocks every healTime seconds. earthWall and iceBridge (optional) let it cast
// Earth Wall and Ice Bridge (see Terrain magic in docs/rules.md).
// Armour (see Combat in docs/rules.md): attack (optional, default slash) is the kind of the unit's attack:
// slash (swords, axes, claws), pierce (spears, arrows, lances) or crush (hammers, clubs, stones, blasts).
// Armour stops slash best and crush least, and slash and pierce wounds bleed. body (optional) is humanoid
// (head, torso, arms, legs; the default) or beast (head, body, legs). armor (optional) is the armour on
// each part, as in { "head": 1, "torso": 2, "arms": 1, "legs": 1 } (a beast's: head, body, legs); each hit
// on a part loses that many points, more against slash and fewer against crush. shield (optional) is the
// percent chance a hit from in front of the unit is stopped whole. bleeds (optional, default true) says
// whether its slash and pierce wounds bleed (see "bleeding" in roles.json); set it false for a body with no
// blood in it.
// start (optional, default true): false keeps a trained unit out of the units matches start with, so it is
// only ever trained (the healer).
// upkeep (optional, default 1) is the food it eats a minute; work (optional, default 100) is how fast
// it gathers, builds, farms and digs tunnels, in percent.
// Evolution: a unit with evolvesFrom is a form that unit can take once it has the experience (see
// "experience" in factions.json): it is not trained, and a form of a form needs one level more. Each
// form gives something and takes something: here every form has one drawback, more upkeep, less speed
// or a weakness (weakTo). A form with fromDen (optional) is not reached through experience: a unit takes it
// only at a cleared monster den, for a faction whose "den" in factions.json names it.
// Monsters: a unit with lair (role monster) lives in the wild, in packs that guard their dens. Each match
// has a lair for every start, on the way to the map centre. pack is the monsters in a lair, guard how
// near its den (blocks) the pack goes after other players' units, leash how far from the den a monster
// chases before it goes back, and reward the goods a player gets for clearing the lair, unless their
// faction tames monsters (see "tamesMonsters" in factions.json): then each monster their units beat is
// theirs to ride or to recruit. A lair with duskTree guards the map's one dusk tree instead (see "Dusk tree" in
// docs/rules.md): it is not placed at the starts and leaves no den.
// biome and count (optional) spread count lairs of the monster over that biome (grassland, forest, hills,
// swamp or beach) for each 512 by 512 blocks of map, well away from the starts, the towns and each other;
// such a monster has no lair at the starts unless atStarts is true. den: false means a cleared lair leaves
// no den to use.
// ride (optional) says which unit can climb on a beaten monster (rider) and what it becomes (becomes); a
// recruited monster fights for its new owner as it is. tracksBlood (optional)
// makes a wild monster follow a fresh blood trail it finds to the bleeding unit, well past its leash.
// City-states (see citystates.json): a unit with the trader role is a caravan, which trades with a
// city-state's town; caravans are trained but no player starts with one. A unit with hiredFrom is a
// mercenary: not trained, but hired for its cost at that city-state's town by the city-state's ally.
